MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m02",
  "summary": "Choose and use hand tools, workshop equipment and measuring instruments correctly, tighten fasteners to specification, and find and use technical information for a vehicle.",
  "outcomes": ["Select the correct spanner, socket, screwdriver or special tool for a fastener and use it without damaging it.","Read a micrometer, vernier calliper and feeler gauge and record a measurement correctly.","Tighten a fastener to specification using a torque wrench, including stages, sequence and angle tightening.","Describe the safe use and care of power tools, air tools, jacks, hoists and workshop equipment.","Locate specifications and procedures in workshop manuals, wiring diagrams and parts catalogues using the vehicle identification number.","Calculate with metric units, ratios, percentages and compression ratio for common workshop tasks."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Use a six-point socket or ring spanner before an open-ended spanner to avoid rounding a nut.","Pull a spanner towards you. Pushing it risks skinned knuckles if it slips.","A torque wrench measures tightening effort. Never use it to loosen a fastener.","Tighten in the sequence in the manual, usually centre outwards or crisscross, in stages.","Micrometer: read the sleeve scale first, then add the thimble reading (each division is 0.01 mm).","Feeler gauges measure small gaps, such as valve clearance, by sliding blades in.","Check the workshop manual for torque, clearance and capacity. Do not guess.","The VIN identifies the exact vehicle, so parts and data match the right model and year.","Compression ratio = (swept volume + clearance volume) ÷ clearance volume."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m02-l1", "title": "Hand tools and fastener basics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson covers the hand tools used most often on vehicles and how to use them without damaging fasteners or yourself."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spanners and sockets"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **spanner** (wrench) turns a nut or bolt head. Spanners are sized by the distance across the flats of the fastener, in millimetres or inches. A 10 mm spanner fits a 10 mm hex head. Using the wrong size, even by a small amount, rounds the corners and ruins the fastener."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Ring spanners** grip all around the head and are less likely to slip than open-ended spanners.","**Combination spanners** have a ring at one end and an open end of the same size at the other.","**Sockets** with a ratchet are fast and grip well. A **six-point** socket grips the flats better than a **twelve-point** socket and is better for tight or rusty fasteners.","**Extension bars** and **universal joints** reach into awkward places, but a joint reduces the torque you can apply.","A **breaker bar** gives extra leverage for loosening tight fasteners. Never use a pipe to extend a ratchet, as it can break the ratchet."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Pull a spanner towards you rather than pushing it. If it slips while you are pulling, your hand moves away from the vehicle. If it slips while you are pushing, your knuckles hit metal."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Screwdrivers and pliers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Screwdriver tips must match the screw head: **slotted**, **Phillips**, **Pozidriv**, **Torx** (star-shaped) and **hex** (Allen). A tip that is too small or worn damages the screw head. Pliers hold and bend; **locking pliers** clamp onto damaged fasteners; **side cutters** cut wire; **circlip pliers** fit snap rings. Do not use pliers on nuts, because they round the corners."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other common tools"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Hammers:** a ball-pein hammer is for metalwork; a soft-faced hammer or rubber mallet is for parts that can be damaged.","**Punches and chisels:** for removing pins and marking positions. Wear eye protection because the heads can chip.","**Pullers:** for removing bearings, gears, hubs and pulleys without hammering.","**Drifts and drivers:** for fitting bearings and seals squarely."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Threads and fasteners"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A thread is described by its **diameter** and **pitch** (the distance from one thread to the next). In metric, M10 × 1.5 means a 10 mm bolt with 1.5 mm between threads. Fine-pitch threads have a smaller pitch value. Metric and imperial threads do not match, and forcing the wrong one destroys both. Check with a thread gauge if unsure."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fastener","Typical use"],"rows":[["Hex bolt and nut","General assembly, suspension, brackets"],["Stud","Held in the block; nut tightens on it, such as on exhaust manifolds and wheels on some vehicles"],["Flange bolt","Spreads the load without a separate washer"],["Torx or hex socket screw","Brake calipers, trims and covers"]],"caption":"Common fasteners and their uses"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing and caring for hand tools"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Good tools save time and protect fasteners. Buy tools made to a recognised standard, use each one for the job it was designed for, and keep them clean and dry. A cheap spanner that opens up under load will round a bolt head and can injure your hand when it slips."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Use a spanner or socket that fits snugly. If it rocks on the head, it is the wrong size or the head is worn.","Push a socket fully onto the fastener and keep the ratchet square to it. A tilted socket slips and rounds the corners.","Wipe oil and grease from handles and from your hands before you use a tool under load.","Do not hammer on ratchets or screwdrivers unless they are made for it. An impact driver is made for hammering. A normal screwdriver is not.","Look at tools for cracks, worn jaws and mushroomed heads, and remove damaged tools from use. A chisel head that has mushroomed can throw off sharp pieces.","Keep precision tools such as torque wrenches and micrometers apart from rough tools, in their own boxes."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Removing damaged fasteners"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Soak the fastener in penetrating oil and wait. Give it time, even overnight if you can.","Tap the head squarely with a hammer to break the rust bond, then try a six-point socket or ring spanner. Try tightening a little first, then loosening.","If the head is rounded, grip it with locking pliers or fit a special socket that bites into rounded heads.","If it still will not move, use a screw extractor, a spiral tool that bites as it turns anticlockwise. Drill a centred pilot hole first.","As a last resort for a broken stud, weld a nut to the stub. The heat helps to free it. Do this only away from fuel, plastic parts, wiring and airbags."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"If a fastener will not turn, stop. Apply penetrating oil, wait, tap it gently, then try again. Forcing a seized bolt usually breaks it, and a broken bolt takes far longer to remove than a stuck one."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l2", "title": "Fasteners, threads, locking devices and sealants", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Every vehicle has thousands of fasteners, and most failures after a repair start with one that was chosen or fitted badly. This lesson explains fastener types and grades, how threads are described, how fasteners are locked in place, and how joints are sealed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Thread types and sizes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A thread is described by its diameter and its **pitch**, the distance between neighbouring threads. A metric bolt called M12 × 1.25 has a 12 mm diameter and a 1.25 mm pitch. A **coarse** thread has a larger pitch for its diameter and a **fine** thread a smaller one. Fine threads grip better in thin or soft metal and resist loosening, and coarse threads are quicker and more tolerant of damage. Imperial threads are described by diameter and the number of threads per inch. Never mix threads, because a wrong thread may start and then strip."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Thread","Used on","Notes"],"rows":[["Metric coarse (M8 × 1.25)","General bolts and screws","Most common on modern vehicles"],["Metric fine (M12 × 1.25)","Spark plugs, sensors, wheel nuts","Used where good grip is needed"],["Tapered pipe thread","Sensors and fittings that seal in the thread","Seals by the taper. Needs a sealant."],["Left-hand thread","Some wheel nuts on old vehicles, some pedal and shaft nuts","Tightens anticlockwise. Check before applying force."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Strength grades"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Bolts are made in different strengths. A metric bolt carries a **property class** on the head, such as 8.8, 10.9 or 12.9. The first number times 100 gives the tensile strength in megapascals (a class 8.8 bolt is about 800 MPa), and the second number shows the yield strength as a fraction of the first. A higher number means a stronger bolt. Imperial bolts are marked with lines on the head: three lines for grade 5 and six lines for grade 8. Never replace a high-strength bolt with a weaker one."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always fit a bolt of the same size, thread and grade as the original. Suspension, steering, brake and engine bolts are high-strength parts chosen for the load they carry."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Locking devices"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Vibration can loosen a fastener, so important fasteners are locked."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Device","How it works","Reuse?"],"rows":[["Split pin (cotter pin)","A pin through a castle nut and the bolt holds the nut from turning","Fit a new one every time"],["Self-locking (nyloc) nut","A nylon ring grips the thread","Replace if loose or after several uses"],["Tab washer","A metal tab is bent up against the nut or bolt head","Fit a new one"],["Spring or serrated washer","Pushes back or bites into the surface","Replace if flattened"],["Thread-locking compound","A liquid that hardens in the thread (light, medium or heavy strength)","Clean and renew the compound"],["Torque-to-yield bolt","Stretched to its limit when tightened","Never reuse"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sealing joints"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Joints that carry oil, coolant, fuel or gas must seal as well as hold. Use the sealing method the manual specifies."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Gaskets** are cut from fibre, metal or rubber and need clean, flat mating faces. Fit them dry or with a light smear of sealant as the manual says.","**Liquid sealants** such as silicone and anaerobic flange sealants fill small gaps. Use the right type for oil, coolant or fuel. Apply a thin, even bead and tighten before the sealant skins over.","**O-rings and seals** are rubber rings that are squeezed into place. Lubricate them with the correct grease or oil, and make sure they are the right material for the fluid.","**Crush washers** are soft copper or aluminium washers that deform to seal sump plugs and banjo bolts. Fit a new one every time.","**Thread tape** and thread sealant are used only on tapered pipe threads, never on parallel threads or on fittings that seal with a washer."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"After removing a part, put the bolts back in the holes or into a labelled tray in order. Bolts of the same appearance can differ in length and grade, and mixing them is a common source of failures."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never fit a wrong-grade or damaged bolt on a wheel, suspension, brake or steering part. A failure can cause loss of control. If you are unsure, use the original part number or the manual."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l3", "title": "Power and workshop equipment", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson covers the power tools and workshop equipment used in a vehicle garage, and how to use and look after them safely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Air and electric tools"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **impact wrench** uses hammering action to loosen or tighten tight fasteners quickly. Air-powered tools need a compressor and the right pressure, usually marked on the tool. Electric tools need undamaged leads and, where possible, a residual-current device. Always check sockets are designed for impact use. Ordinary chrome sockets can shatter."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use an impact wrench to tighten wheel nuts or critical fasteners to final torque. Use it to run them on, then finish with a torque wrench."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The compressed air system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A workshop compressor stores air in a tank and feeds it through pipes and hoses to the tools. Air pressure is usually set around 6 to 8 bar (90 to 115 psi), and each tool has its own maximum, shown on a label. Too much pressure damages the tool or makes it unsafe."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Drain water from the compressor tank each day. Water causes rust inside the tank and ruins air tools.","Check hoses and couplings for cuts, bulges and leaks. Replace damaged hoses.","Fit safety clips or whip checks on hose joints so a broken coupling cannot whip about.","Switch off the air supply and release the pressure before you change a tool or work on a hose.","Check the pressure relief valve regularly. It protects the tank from bursting.","Use an air line oiler or put a few drops of air-tool oil in the inlet daily when the tool maker says so."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Grinders, drills and presses"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Bench grinder:** keep the tool rest within 3 mm of the wheel and wear eye protection. Never grind aluminium on a stone made for steel.","**Pedestal drill:** clamp small parts, never hold them by hand, and remove the chuck key before starting.","**Hydraulic press:** use the right support blocks and keep fingers out of the pinch area. Parts can fly if they fail."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Lifting and handling equipment"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Trolley jacks** lift one end of a vehicle. **Axle stands** support it. An **engine hoist** (crane) lifts an engine; its capacity changes with how far the boom is extended, so check the chart on the hoist. **Transmission jacks** hold a gearbox when it is lowered. Inspect lifting gear before each use. Do not use damaged chains, slings or hooks."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Workshop equipment"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Equipment","Care and safety"],"rows":[["Parts washer","Use the correct solvent; keep the lid closed; wear gloves"],["Battery charger","Ventilate the area; set to the right voltage; connect before switching on"],["Compressor","Drain water from the tank daily; check pressure relief valve"],["Wheel balancer and tyre changer","Train before use; keep hands clear of moving parts"],["Welding equipment","Use correct shielding and screens; check for fuel and fire risks nearby"]],"caption":"Routine care and safety for workshop equipment"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Welding and heating"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Workshop welding is mostly **MIG welding** (a wire electrode fed through a gun, with shielding gas), plus gas brazing and heating with a torch. These need training. Fumes, ultraviolet light and sparks cause burns, eye damage and fires. Always wear a welding helmet or shield, gloves and flame-resistant clothing, and screen the area so other people are not exposed to the arc."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Never weld or heat near fuel lines, tanks, plastic parts, wiring, airbags or gas struts without removing or shielding them.","Disconnect the battery and protect electronic modules before you weld. An arc can send a surge through the vehicle's electronics.","Do not weld or heat parts the maker says must not be welded, such as many high-strength steel structural parts. The heat weakens them and they can fail in a crash.","Keep a fire extinguisher at hand and check the area for smouldering when you finish."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Looking after tools"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Clean tools after use and return them to their place. A tool that is missing or damaged should be reported, not hidden. Good tools last for years if they are kept dry and used for the job they were made for. A screwdriver used as a chisel or a pry bar will bend or snap."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Battery chargers and jump packs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **battery charger** forces current back into a discharged battery. A **jump pack** is a small battery that can start an engine. Both can cause sparks, burns and explosions if they are connected wrongly. Check the voltage, connect to the battery before switching on, and remove the clamps with the charger switched off. Never leave a charger running on a battery that gets hot, or in a closed space. Hydrogen gas collects around a charging battery."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Before using any power tool, check three things: the guard is in place, the cable or hose is undamaged, and you know how to switch it off quickly."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l4", "title": "Test and diagnostic equipment", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Modern diagnosis relies on instruments that show what a system is doing. This lesson introduces the main test equipment and what each tells you. Later modules show how to use them in detail. The point here is to know what each tool is for, and its limits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Meters and lamps"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **digital multimeter** measures voltage (V), resistance (Ω) and current (A), and often continuity, frequency and temperature. It is the most-used diagnostic tool. A **clamp meter** measures current without breaking the circuit by closing around a cable. A **test lamp** shows that voltage is present. Use only an LED-type or high-resistance test lamp on electronic circuits. A traditional filament lamp draws too much current and can damage control units and must never be used on airbag circuits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Scan tools and code readers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **scan tool** connects to the diagnostic socket and talks to the vehicle's control modules. It can read **diagnostic trouble codes** (DTCs), freeze-frame data that records conditions when a code was set, and live data from sensors. Some can run actuator tests, adapt or relearn components and update software. A basic code reader only reads and clears codes. A code points to a circuit or system to test, not directly to a faulty part."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oscilloscope"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **oscilloscope** draws voltage against time, so it shows how a signal changes, for example a crankshaft sensor waveform or an injector pulse. It finds faults that a meter averages out, such as short drop-outs and noise. Scopes take practice, and a few recorded known-good waveforms are very helpful for comparison."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mechanical test equipment"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Tool","What it measures","Typical use"],"rows":[["Compression tester","Peak cylinder pressure while cranking","Engine mechanical condition"],["Leak-down tester","How much of a pressurised cylinder leaks, and where","Finding valve, ring or gasket leaks"],["Vacuum gauge","Intake manifold vacuum","Engine and exhaust restriction checks"],["Oil pressure gauge","Lubrication pressure","Pump and bearing condition"],["Fuel pressure gauge","Pressure in the fuel rail","Fuel pump and regulator tests"],["Cooling system pressure tester","Pressure held by the cooling system","Leaks and cap test"],["Manifold gauge set","High and low side refrigerant pressure","Air conditioning diagnosis"],["Smoke machine","Shows leaks by smoke escaping","Vacuum, intake and evaporative leaks"],["Exhaust gas analyser","CO, HC, CO2 and O2 in exhaust","Combustion and emission checks"],["Battery conductance tester","The battery's ability to deliver current","Battery health"],["Thermal camera or thermometer","Surface temperature","Dragging brakes, blocked radiators, misfires"],["Stethoscope","Sound from a part","Locating noises in bearings, injectors and pumps"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using test equipment well"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Know what the tool measures, its range and its limits before you connect it.","Check it first on something you know to be good, such as a battery, to be sure it reads correctly.","Connect leads securely and keep them away from moving parts and hot surfaces.","Compare readings with a specification, not with a feeling.","Look after meters and leads. A damaged lead or flat battery causes false readings."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing the right instrument"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Start from the question you need to answer, then pick the tool. Is there voltage at this connector? Use a meter. Does the signal look right while the engine runs? Use a scope. Is this cylinder sealing? Use a compression or leak-down test. Does the fault memory hold a code? Use a scan tool. Beginning with the cheapest, simplest check saves time. Many faults are found with a meter and a careful look, long before an expensive tool is needed."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Question","First tool to try"],"rows":[["Is power or earth reaching the part?","Multimeter or test lamp"],["What does the control module think is wrong?","Scan tool"],["Is the engine mechanically healthy?","Compression tester, vacuum gauge"],["Is air getting in where it should not?","Smoke machine"],["Is a sensor signal clean and in time?","Oscilloscope"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Test equipment gives data. It does not give answers. You must know what the normal value is, read the result and decide what it means."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use a standard test lamp or an ohmmeter on airbag circuits. They can fire the airbag. Use only the scan tool or the tool the manual specifies."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l5", "title": "Measuring tools", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson explains how to read the main measuring tools used on engines and chassis parts: the steel rule, vernier calliper, micrometer, dial gauge and feeler gauge."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Rule and vernier calliper"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **steel rule** measures to about 1 mm. A **vernier calliper** measures outside, inside and depth dimensions, usually to 0.02 mm or 0.05 mm. Read the main scale at the zero of the sliding scale, then add the first line on the vernier scale that lines up with a line on the main scale. Digital callipers show the number directly, but still check the zero before use."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a 0.02 mm vernier calliper","steps":["The zero of the sliding scale has passed the 24 mm line on the main scale, so the whole millimetres are 24.","Look along the vernier scale for the line that lines up exactly with any main scale line. Suppose it is line number 14.","Each vernier line is worth 0.02 mm, so 14 × 0.02 = 0.28 mm.","Add them together: 24 + 0.28 = 24.28 mm."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The outside micrometer"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **micrometer** measures to 0.01 mm and is more accurate than a calliper. Its spindle moves forward 0.5 mm for each full turn of the thimble. The sleeve scale shows whole millimetres on one side and half millimetres on the other, and the thimble is divided into 50 parts, each worth 0.01 mm."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Reading = sleeve reading + thimble reading","caption":"For 7.5 mm on the sleeve and 38 on the thimble: 7.50 + 0.38 = 7.88 mm"},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"micrometer-reading"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Clean the part and the anvil and spindle faces.","Close the micrometer gently with the ratchet and check it reads zero.","Hold the part and close the micrometer with the ratchet until it clicks, so the pressure is always the same.","Read the sleeve, then the thimble. Add them together and record the result with its units."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always close a micrometer with the ratchet or friction thimble. Tightening by hand squeezes the part and gives a reading that is too small."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading an outside micrometer","steps":["The sleeve shows 17 whole millimetres and the half-millimetre line is also visible, so the sleeve reading is 17.5 mm.","The thimble line number 23 is level with the datum line on the sleeve, so the thimble reading is 23 × 0.01 = 0.23 mm.","Add them: 17.5 + 0.23 = 17.73 mm.","If the half-millimetre line is not visible, the sleeve reading would be 17.0 mm and the result 17.23 mm. Always check whether the half-millimetre line is showing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dial gauges and feeler gauges"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **dial gauge** (dial indicator) measures small movements, such as crankshaft end float, wheel runout or gear backlash. Mount it firmly on a stand, set the needle to zero against the surface, then move the part and read the change. **Feeler gauges** are thin steel blades of known thickness used to measure small gaps, such as valve clearance and spark plug gaps. Choose the blade that slides in with light drag."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure and vacuum gauges"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Pressure gauges test oil pressure, fuel pressure and compression. A **compression tester** screws into the spark plug hole to measure how much pressure each cylinder makes while cranking. A **leak-down tester** measures how much of the pressure leaks away, and where. A **vacuum gauge** on the intake manifold shows engine condition. Pressure is given in kPa, bar or psi: 1 bar is about 100 kPa or 14.5 psi."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Tool","Typical accuracy","Used for"],"rows":[["Steel rule","1 mm","Rough lengths and distances"],["Vernier or digital calliper","0.02 mm","Shaft, bore and depth measurements"],["Micrometer","0.01 mm","Crankshaft journals, piston diameter, discs"],["Feeler gauge","0.01 mm steps","Valve clearance, gaps"]],"caption":"Common measuring tools"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring bores and shafts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A micrometer measures the outside of a part. To measure the inside of a hole you can use an **inside micrometer**, a **bore gauge**, or a **telescoping gauge** that you lock in the bore and then measure with a micrometer. A **depth micrometer** or the depth rod of a calliper measures the depth of a hole or step. Measure across the bore in two directions at three heights, as the next lessons explain."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other measuring aids"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Straight edge and feeler gauge:** check that a cylinder head or manifold face is flat by laying the straight edge on it and trying to slide a feeler blade underneath.","**Plastigauge:** a soft plastic thread, laid across a bearing journal and crushed when the cap is tightened. The width of the flattened thread is compared with a chart to read the bearing clearance.","**Dial gauge on a magnetic stand:** measures runout of a shaft, disc or flywheel as it turns, and end float of a shaft.","**Thread pitch gauge:** a set of blades that fit the threads to read the pitch.","**Angle gauge:** used for torque-plus-angle tightening, where the angle matters as much as the torque."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always clean the measuring faces and the part before you measure, and measure at a temperature near 20 °C where possible. A film of oil or a hot part spoils the reading."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l6", "title": "Torque and tightening", "minutes": 7, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson explains why tightening to the correct torque matters and how to use a torque wrench properly. Over-tightened and under-tightened fasteners are a leading cause of failures."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What torque does"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Torque** is a turning force, measured in **newton metres** (N·m) or pound-feet (lbf·ft). A fastener stretches slightly when tightened, like a spring, and that stretch clamps the parts together. Too little stretch and the joint can loosen or leak. Too much and the bolt stretches permanently, strips its thread or breaks."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Torque turns into clamp force through the thread, but most of it is lost to friction. In a typical dry steel bolt, about 90% of the torque you apply is used to overcome friction under the head and in the thread, and only about 10% stretches the bolt. This is why oil, rust or dirt on a thread changes the clamp force so much. A rough relationship is:"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"T = K × D × F","caption":"T is torque in N·m, D is the bolt diameter in metres, F is the clamp force in newtons, and K is the nut factor, about 0.2 for a dry steel thread"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why oil changes the clamp force","steps":["An M10 bolt (D = 0.010 m) is tightened to 50 N·m with a dry thread, so K is about 0.2. Clamp force F = T ÷ (K × D) = 50 ÷ (0.2 × 0.010) = 25,000 N, or 25 kN.","Oil the same thread and K falls to about 0.15. Now F = 50 ÷ (0.15 × 0.010) = 33,300 N, about 33 kN.","The same torque gives a third more clamp force, enough to overstress the bolt. That is why you must follow the manual about whether to oil threads."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using a torque wrench"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Set the required value. Click-type wrenches click or give way when the torque is reached; stop pulling at once.","Use a smooth, steady pull. Do not jerk or use an extension that changes the lever length without allowing for it.","Keep the thread clean and dry unless the manual says to oil it, because oil on a thread can make the fastener tighten more than intended.","Return a click-type wrench to its lowest setting after use so the spring does not weaken.","Never use a torque wrench to loosen a fastener."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of torque wrench"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Care"],"rows":[["Click type","Clicks and gives slightly when the set torque is reached","Wind back to the lowest setting after use. Do not use it to loosen."],["Beam type","A pointer moves along a scale as the beam bends","Read the scale square on. Check the pointer returns to zero."],["Dial type","A dial shows the torque during the pull","Keep it from knocks. Zero the pointer before use."],["Digital type","An electronic reading, often with a beep or light at the target","Keep the battery charged. Store it dry."]],"caption":"All types should be checked against a standard at set intervals, commonly once a year or after a drop."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Using an extension on a torque wrench","steps":["A torque wrench has an effective length L of 0.40 m. A 0.10 m extension in line with the wrench lengthens the lever, so the torque at the bolt is greater than the wrench shows.","The wrench setting is T_wrench = T_wanted × L ÷ (L + E).","For a wanted torque of 100 N·m: T_wrench = 100 × 0.40 ÷ (0.40 + 0.10) = 80 N·m. Set the wrench to 80 N·m, so the bolt gets 100 N·m.","If the extension is at right angles to the wrench, the torque is not changed and the setting stays the same."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sequence and stages"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Parts held by several bolts, such as a cylinder head, wheel or sump, must be tightened evenly. Follow the order in the manual. A rough guide is: start in the centre and work outwards in a spiral for large covers, or tighten opposite pairs in a crisscross pattern for a wheel or flange. Tighten in stages, for example hand tight, then half torque, then full torque."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"tightening-sequence"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Torque plus angle"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern engine bolts are **torque-to-yield**. The manual gives a torque followed by an angle, for example 30 N·m plus 90°. The angle stretches the bolt into a controlled range. These bolts may only be used once and must be replaced after removal."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Wheel nut torque","steps":["The manual says 110 N·m for the wheel nuts.","Tighten the nuts by hand until they touch the wheel.","Tighten in a crisscross order to about half torque, 55 N·m.","Repeat to the full 110 N·m in the same crisscross order.","After driving about 50 km, check the torque again, as the wheel can settle."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common tightening mistakes"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Using the wrong torque value, for example from a different engine.","Tightening on dirty, rusty or damaged threads, so that the torque is lost to friction.","Tightening a fastener in one go instead of in stages and in sequence, so a flange or head warps.","Reusing torque-to-yield bolts, which have already been stretched.","Using an impact wrench for final tightening, or a torque wrench as a breaker bar.","Not checking after a short run when the manual asks for a re-torque."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a thread is damaged, repair it instead of tightening it further. A **helical thread insert**, a coil of stainless wire fitted into a re-tapped hole, restores the original thread size. Follow the kit instructions, and use the correct tap and driver."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Signs of wrong tightening"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Too loose:** a joint that weeps oil or coolant, a noise that comes and goes, fretting marks around a bolt, or a nut that has backed off.","**Too tight:** a stretched or necked bolt, a stripped thread, a cracked casting or housing, a distorted gasket face, or a squashed seal.","**Uneven:** a cylinder head, flange or brake disc that has distorted, with leaks on one side or a judder."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Soft materials and torque units"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Aluminium, magnesium and plastic parts strip or crack easily, so tighten them with a torque wrench and to the lower values in the manual. Spark plugs and sump plugs are common examples. Use the unit the manual gives. To convert, multiply newton metres by 0.74 to get pound-feet, or pound-feet by 1.356 to get newton metres. A torque of 110 N·m is about 81 lbf·ft."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never tighten wheel nuts only with an impact wrench. Uneven or excessive tightening can distort a brake disc or break a stud, and loose wheel nuts can cause a wheel to come off while driving."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Torque values depend on the bolt size, grade, thread condition and the part it holds. Always take the value from the workshop manual for that vehicle, not from memory."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l7", "title": "Dismantling, inspecting and reassembling good practice", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Skilled technicians work in an orderly way. They keep parts in order, keep dirt out, replace what must be replaced and check their work. These habits matter more than speed, because a rushed dismantling and a careless rebuild cause most comebacks, jobs that return because they failed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Before you start"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Read the procedure in the manual and note the special tools, torque values and any parts that must be replaced.","Make sure you have the tools, the parts, the gaskets and the fluids before you take anything apart.","Make the vehicle safe: chock it, support it properly, disconnect the battery, and drain fluids into containers.","Clean the outside of the unit before you open it. Dirt that falls inside causes wear."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"During dismantling"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Take photographs of the assembly and of connectors and hoses before you disconnect them.","Label wires, hoses and connectors, and mark parts that must be refitted in the same position, such as pistons, valves, bearing caps, shims and gears.","Place fasteners in labelled bags or a divided tray, in the order you remove them.","Cover open pipes, ports and fuel lines with caps or clean plugs so that dirt cannot enter.","Do not use hammers or levers on parts that are not meant for them. If a part does not come off, look for a hidden bolt before you apply force."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cleaning and inspecting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Clean parts with the right solvent or washer and dry them with clean air or lint-free rags. Then examine each one. Look for cracks, scoring, wear, discolouration from heat, distortion, and damaged threads. Measure critical parts against the manual limits and record the results. If you have any doubt about a safety-critical part, replace it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parts you should always replace"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Gaskets, O-rings, oil seals and crush washers.","Split pins, tab washers and circlips that are bent or worn.","Torque-to-yield bolts and self-locking nuts that the manual says are single-use.","Filters, and timing belts and tensioners when the job calls for them.","Any part that was exposed to oil contamination, overheating or impact damage."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reassembly"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check that every part is clean, and lubricate moving parts with the correct oil or grease.","Fit gaskets and seals dry or with the sealant the manual specifies. Seals are pressed in square with a driver, lip towards the fluid, and never hammered in by hand.","Fit bearings by pressing on the ring that has the tight fit, which is the inner ring for a shaft and the outer ring for a housing. Never push through the rolling elements.","Tighten fasteners in the correct sequence and in stages to the specified torque, following any angle instructions.","Refill with the correct fluids to the correct level, and bleed any air from hydraulic or cooling systems.","Reconnect the battery, start the engine if appropriate, and check for leaks, noise and warning lights."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Final checks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Before the vehicle leaves, double-check the work. Look at every fastener you touched. Make sure no tools or rags are left in the engine bay. Take a short road test if it applies. Update the job card with the work done, the parts fitted and any recommendations. Clean the vehicle where you left marks, and put your tools away."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Special tools and workshop habits"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many jobs need a special tool: a seal driver, a bearing puller, a pulley holder, a spring compressor or a timing tool set. Using the right tool avoids damage and is faster than improvising. If you have no special tool, ask whether you can borrow or hire one before you try a workaround. A homemade tool that slips can ruin a part or hurt you."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Keep a clean work area. Put a clean cloth or mat under parts, wipe up fluid as you go and keep dirty tools away from clean assemblies. Count your tools before you close up a job. A spanner or rag left in an engine can cause a very expensive failure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A job is not finished when the last bolt is tight. It is finished when it has been checked, tested and recorded."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep the old parts until the customer has seen them or the job is signed off. They show what was replaced and can help to find the cause of a failure."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l8", "title": "Technical information", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson explains where to find reliable technical information for a vehicle and how to use it. Good technicians do not guess. They look it up."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Identifying the vehicle"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **vehicle identification number** (VIN) is a 17-character code that is unique to each vehicle. It is stamped on the body and shown on a plate under the bonnet or in the windscreen corner. The VIN tells you the maker, model year, plant and engine type, so the right parts and data can be selected. Always check the VIN before ordering parts."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **vehicle data plate** also shows paint, trim, engine and transmission codes. Record them on the job card."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The 17 characters of the VIN are not random. The first three identify the maker and country. The next six describe the model, body, engine and restraint type. The tenth character is the model year, and the eleventh is the assembly plant. The last six are the serial number. A VIN with a mistyped character will not find the vehicle in a parts system, so copy it carefully from the vehicle, not from the registration papers alone."},
      {"t":"table","head":["VIN position","What it tells you"],"rows":[["1 to 3","World manufacturer identifier: maker and country of origin"],["4 to 9","Vehicle descriptor: model, body type, engine, restraint system, check digit"],["10","Model year"],["11","Assembly plant"],["12 to 17","Production serial number"]],"caption":"The exact layout of positions 4 to 9 differs between makers and between regions."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Workshop manuals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **workshop manual** (service manual) contains procedures, specifications, torque values, fluid capacities and fault-finding charts. It is written for one vehicle range, and the engine and market matter. Used imports can differ from local models, so make sure the manual matches the engine code and year."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Specifications:** clearances, capacities, pressures, torque values.","**Procedures:** step-by-step removal and refitting, with special tool numbers.","**Diagnostic charts:** symptom-based test sequences.","**Wiring diagrams:** circuits, connectors, fuses and earth points."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parts catalogues and bulletins"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **parts catalogue** has exploded diagrams with part numbers. It helps you order the correct part and see how a unit fits together. **Service bulletins** are notices from makers about known faults and updated procedures. They are often the quickest route to a fix for a common problem."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Digital information and scan tools"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many workshops use online or software-based information systems. They give procedures, wiring diagrams, codes and data. A **scan tool** reads fault codes and live data from the vehicle's control modules. Always check that the information is for the right vehicle, and note the source on the job card."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Question","Where to look"],"rows":[["What torque for this bolt?","Workshop manual specifications"],["Which wire goes to the horn?","Wiring diagram"],["Which part number do I order?","Parts catalogue and the VIN"],["Is this a known fault?","Service bulletins and the maker's information system"]],"caption":"Finding the right information"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Choosing reliable information"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Not all information is equal. Use the maker's workshop manual or an approved information system as your first choice, because it covers the exact vehicle and is checked. A general aftermarket manual is a good second source. Forum posts and videos can give useful ideas, but never take a torque value, a specification or a safety procedure from them without checking it in a reliable source."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the model year, engine code and market. A specification for a European model may not fit the same car sold elsewhere.","Look for **service bulletins**, **recalls** and **campaigns**, which announce known faults or required repairs.","Note down the document name and the date on the job card so the information can be traced.","If two sources disagree, find out why, and use the maker's data."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a service schedule"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **service schedule** lists what to do at each distance or time interval, such as engine oil and filter, air filter, spark plugs, brake fluid, coolant and timing belts. The shorter of the distance and time interval applies. Hard use, such as dusty roads, short journeys, towing or very hot weather, calls for shorter intervals. Record what you did, the date and the odometer reading so the next service can be planned."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph or write down the VIN and the codes on the data plate at the start of a job. It saves time when ordering parts or looking up data."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l9", "title": "Measuring and calculating", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson covers the units and calculations you will use daily in vehicle work: metric prefixes, tolerances, ratios and the compression ratio."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Units and prefixes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicle work uses millimetres (mm), litres (L), kilopascals (kPa), bar, degrees Celsius, newton metres and revolutions per minute (rpm). Prefixes change the size of a unit: **kilo** (k) means 1,000, **milli** (m) means one thousandth, and **micro** (µ) means one millionth. 1 m = 1,000 mm. 1 L = 1,000 mL. 1 bar = 100 kPa."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Convert","Value"],"rows":[["Inches to millimetres","1 in = 25.4 mm"],["Bar to psi","1 bar ≈ 14.5 psi"],["N·m to lbf·ft","1 N·m ≈ 0.74 lbf·ft"],["Litres to US gallons","1 L ≈ 0.264 US gal"]],"caption":"Common conversions"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tolerances and percentages"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **tolerance** is the allowed difference from a specified size. A bore specified as 80.00 mm with a limit of 0.05 mm wear means a reading above 80.05 mm is out of limit. A percentage compares a part to a whole: 5% of 80 mm is 4 mm."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ratios"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **gear ratio** is the number of teeth on the driven gear divided by the number on the driving gear. A driver with 20 teeth turning a driven gear with 60 teeth gives 3:1. The driven gear turns one third as fast, but with three times the torque. **Pulley ratios** work the same way with diameters."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Final drive ratio","steps":["The pinion has 10 teeth and the crown wheel has 41 teeth.","Ratio = 41 ÷ 10 = 4.1 to 1.","At 4,100 rpm of the propeller shaft, the wheels turn at 4,100 ÷ 4.1 = 1,000 rpm."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression ratio"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **compression ratio** is how much the engine squeezes the air and fuel. It compares the volume above the piston at the bottom of its stroke to the volume at the top."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"CR = (swept volume + clearance volume) ÷ clearance volume","caption":"Swept volume is the volume the piston moves through. Clearance volume is what is left at the top."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Compression ratio of one cylinder","steps":["Swept volume is 500 cm³ and clearance volume is 50 cm³.","Total volume = 500 + 50 = 550 cm³.","CR = 550 ÷ 50 = 11 to 1."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Swept volume and engine size"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The size of an engine is its **swept volume**, the volume the pistons sweep between the top and bottom of their strokes, added over all the cylinders. A cylinder is a circular tube, so its volume is the area of the circle times the stroke."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Swept volume of one cylinder = π ÷ 4 × bore² × stroke","caption":"Use the same unit for bore and stroke. Multiply by the number of cylinders for the engine capacity."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Engine capacity of a four-cylinder engine","steps":["The bore is 80 mm (8.0 cm) and the stroke is 90 mm (9.0 cm).","One cylinder: 0.7854 × 8.0² × 9.0 = 0.7854 × 64 × 9.0 = 452.4 cm³.","Four cylinders: 4 × 452.4 = 1,809.6 cm³, which is sold as a 1.8 L engine."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Power, torque and speed"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Power** is how fast work is done, measured in kilowatts (kW). Engines are tested for torque at different speeds. Power is torque multiplied by rotational speed:"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Power (kW) = torque (N·m) × engine speed (rpm) ÷ 9549"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Power at 3,000 rpm","steps":["An engine produces 200 N·m of torque at 3,000 rpm.","Power = 200 × 3,000 ÷ 9,549 = 62.8 kW.","One kilowatt is about 1.34 horsepower, so this is about 84 horsepower."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed from wheel rpm"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Road speed from wheel speed","steps":["A 205/55 R16 tyre has a diameter of about 632 mm, so the circumference is π × 0.632 = 1.985 m.","At 800 rpm the wheel covers 800 × 1.985 = 1,588 m each minute.","Speed = 1,588 × 60 ÷ 1,000 = 95.3 km/h."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Force from pressure"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Fluid pressure acting on an area makes a force. This is the basis of hydraulic brakes, clutches and presses. Use the same units: 1 bar is the same as 0.1 N per mm²."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Force on a piston","steps":["A brake line pressure of 10 bar (1 N/mm²) acts on a piston 50 mm in diameter.","Area = 0.7854 × 50² = 1,963 mm².","Force = 1 N/mm² × 1,963 mm² = 1,963 N, nearly 200 kg of force."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel consumption"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Measure","Meaning","Example for 8.0 L/100 km"],"rows":[["L/100 km","Litres used to travel 100 km. Lower is better.","8.0"],["km per litre","Kilometres per litre. Higher is better. Divide 100 by the L/100 km figure.","100 ÷ 8.0 = 12.5 km/L"],["Miles per gallon (UK)","Divide 282.5 by the L/100 km figure","35.3 mpg"],["Miles per gallon (US)","Divide 235.2 by the L/100 km figure","29.4 mpg"]],"caption":"UK and US gallons differ in size, so UK and US mpg figures differ."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always write the unit with every number and check that all quantities in a calculation use the same unit before you start. Mixing cm³ and litres is a common source of error."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m02-l10", "title": "Using information in the workshop", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson explains how to keep notes, record measurements and use information correctly so your work can be checked and repeated."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Record as you go"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Write down measurements and findings during a job, not afterwards. Include the date, the vehicle details, the reading, the specification and the result. If a bore is out of limit, record both the reading and the limit. This protects you and gives the next technician something to work from."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking a measurement"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Measure twice. If the two readings differ, find out why before going on.","Check your instrument against a known standard, such as a gauge block or the zero position, before use.","Measure at the right temperature. Metal expands when hot, so measure engine parts when they are cool unless the manual says otherwise.","Measure in more than one place on a round part to detect taper and out-of-round wear."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Taper and out-of-round"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A worn cylinder bore is usually wider at the top, where the ring and piston loads are highest. The difference between the top and bottom readings is called **taper**. If readings across the bore at right angles differ, the bore is **out of round**. Both are signs of wear."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading a bore","steps":["Measure the bore at the top, middle and bottom, across the engine and along it.","Top readings are 80.04 mm and 80.01 mm. Bottom readings are 80.00 mm and 80.00 mm.","Taper = 80.04 − 80.00 = 0.04 mm. Out of round at the top = 80.04 − 80.01 = 0.03 mm.","Compare these with the limit in the manual. If either is over the limit, the bore needs machining or the engine needs replacing."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Calibration and care"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Torque wrenches, gauges and meters need to be checked against a standard regularly. A tool that has been dropped or abused may read wrongly. Store precision tools in their boxes and keep them clean. If a tool is not in good condition, replace or recalibrate it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"New size, service limit and wear limit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A manual often lists three values for a wearing part. The **new size** is the nominal value with its tolerance. The **service limit** (wear limit) is the largest wear allowed before the part must be replaced or repaired. Between the two the part is still fit for use. A measurement above the wear limit means action."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Value","Example for a cylinder bore","Action"],"rows":[["New size","80.00 mm, plus 0.02 mm tolerance","Part is as made"],["Wear limit","80.08 mm maximum","Replace or machine if the reading is above"],["Measured","80.05 mm","Within the limit. Record and keep going."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Writing a good measurement record"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A good record states what was measured, where, with which tool, and the result with its unit. Another technician should be able to repeat it. For example: \"No. 2 main journal, micrometer 50 to 75 mm, 54.97 mm and 54.98 mm at 90 degrees, limit 54.94 mm: within limit.\" Avoid notes such as \"OK\" or \"worn\" with no figures."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading numbers carefully"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Write down every digit your instrument gives, including zeros, because 80.50 mm is not the same claim as 80.5 mm. Do not round until the end of a calculation. When you copy a value from the manual, check the unit and the decimal point: 0.25 mm and 2.5 mm differ by a factor of ten, and the wrong one can ruin an engine. If a figure looks very different from what you expect, measure again before you act on it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a measurement does not seem right, stop and check the tool before assuming the part is faulty. A worn instrument or a dirty anvil gives wrong readings."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Measure parts and tighten to specification","objective":"Use a micrometer, calliper and feeler gauge on a training part, then tighten a flange or cover in the correct sequence using a torque wrench.","time":"60 minutes","tools":["Outside micrometer (0 to 25 mm) and vernier or digital calliper","Feeler gauge set and a steel rule","Torque wrench with a socket set","A training part such as a piston, a crankshaft journal or a cover with several bolts","Workshop manual or a printed specification sheet and a notepad"],"safety":["Wear safety glasses and keep the work area tidy.","Handle precision instruments gently and do not use them as a hammer.","Never use a torque wrench to loosen a fastener.","Clamp or support the part so it cannot fall when you measure it.","Wipe up oil so the floor and tools are not slippery."],"steps":["Clean the training part and the measuring faces of the micrometer and calliper.","Close the micrometer on its anvil with the ratchet and check it reads zero.","Measure the part diameter at three places with the micrometer. Record each reading with its unit.","Measure the same part with the calliper and compare it with the micrometer reading.","Use the steel rule to measure the overall length and note how its accuracy differs.","Use the feeler gauge to measure a gap given by your instructor and record the blade thickness.","Find the torque and tightening order for the cover or flange in the manual.","Fit the bolts by hand and snug them gently in the correct sequence.","Set the torque wrench to half the final value and tighten all the bolts in sequence.","Set the torque wrench to the full value and tighten again in sequence, stopping at the click.","Compare your measurements with the specification and write whether the part is in limits.","Return the torque wrench to its lowest setting and put all tools away clean."],"checks":["I can read a micrometer to 0.01 mm and record the unit.","I can use a feeler gauge to measure a small gap.","I can use a torque wrench to tighten bolts in the correct sequence and in stages.","I can explain why a torque wrench must never be used to loosen a fastener.","I can find a specification in a workshop manual and compare it with my reading."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m02-q01","q":"Which socket type grips a rusty or tight bolt head best?","options":["An adjustable spanner, set tightly on the corners of the head","A six-point socket","Locking pliers clamped onto the corners of the rusty head","A twelve-point socket, which has more contact points on the head"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l1","explain":"A six-point socket holds more of the flat area of the head and is less likely to round the corners than a twelve-point socket."},
    {"id":"m02-q02","q":"You push on a spanner and it slips. Why is pulling the spanner towards you safer?","options":["Spanners are designed to be used only in one direction, towards the user","If it slips, your hand moves away from the vehicle instead of hitting it","Pulling gives you more torque than pushing, so the nut turns more easily","Pulling stops the spanner from rounding the corners of the nut or bolt"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l1","explain":"Pulling keeps your hand moving away from hard surfaces if the tool slips. It does not change the torque."},
    {"id":"m02-q03","q":"What does M10 × 1.5 describe?","options":["A bolt that is 10 mm long with a head size of 1.5 mm across the flats","A bolt with 10 threads in total and a head that is 1.5 mm thick","A metric bolt of 10 mm diameter with a thread pitch of 1.5 mm","A bolt of 10 mm thread diameter that is tightened to 1.5 N·m of torque"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l1","explain":"The first number is the nominal diameter and the second is the distance between threads."},
    {"id":"m02-q04","q":"What does the pitch of a thread mean?","options":["The angle at which the head of the bolt is cut","The distance from one thread crest to the next","The overall length of the threaded part of the bolt","The number of times the bolt can be reused safely"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l1","explain":"Pitch is the distance between neighbouring threads. A metric size such as M12 × 1.25 gives the diameter in millimetres and the pitch in millimetres."},
    {"id":"m02-q05","q":"A bolt is seized and will not turn. What should you do first?","options":["Add a long pipe to the spanner and force it with all your weight","Heat it with a gas torch immediately, whatever is near the bolt","Hit the spanner repeatedly with a heavy hammer until it moves","Apply penetrating oil, wait, then tap the head and try again"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l1","explain":"Time and penetrating oil often free a seized bolt. Forcing it usually snaps the bolt, and heating without care can ignite fuel or damage plastic and wiring."},
    {"id":"m02-q06","q":"What does the number 10.9 on a bolt head tell you?","options":["It is a thread size of 10.9 millimetres across the head","It is the weight of the bolt measured in grams exactly","A metric strength class, stronger than a class 8.8 bolt","It is the torque in newton metres that it should receive"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l2","explain":"The two numbers form a property class. The first times 100 gives the tensile strength in megapascals, so class 10.9 is about 1,000 MPa and is stronger than 8.8."},
    {"id":"m02-q07","q":"Which of these must always be replaced when it has been removed?","options":["A split pin or a copper crush washer","A bolt with a hexagon head and a clean thread","A steel spanner that has a few scratches on it","A plain flat washer in perfect condition"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l2","explain":"Split pins and crush washers deform when used and cannot be relied on a second time. Fit new ones every time."},
    {"id":"m02-q08","q":"What is thread tape used for?","options":["Locking any bolt that tends to come loose on its own","Sealing the flat face of every flange and gasket joint","Sealing tapered pipe threads only","Protecting the thread of a bolt from rust while it is stored"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l2","explain":"Thread tape fills the gap in a tapered thread. It should not be used on parallel threads or on fittings that seal with a washer, because pieces can break off and block small passages."},
    {"id":"m02-q09","q":"Why can a bolt tightened to the same torque give a different clamp force after oil is applied to the thread?","options":["Less friction, so more of the torque stretches the bolt","Oil makes the metal of the bolt harder and more brittle","Oil makes the nut fit more loosely so that it turns freely","Oil changes the thread pitch very slightly under load"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l2","explain":"Most of the torque is used to overcome friction. Less friction means more of the torque becomes clamp force, which can overstress the bolt."},
    {"id":"m02-q10","q":"A bolt is seized and will not turn. What is the best first action?","options":["Hit the bolt head hard with a hammer until the rust breaks free","Heat the bolt with a torch, even if fuel lines are close by","Use a long pipe on the spanner and pull hard until the bolt moves","Apply penetrating oil, wait, and then try again with a gentle tap"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"Forcing a seized bolt tends to snap it. Penetrating oil and patience usually works."},
    {"id":"m02-q11","q":"A parts washer is used with a solvent. Which action is correct?","options":["Use petrol instead of the solvent for better cleaning","Wash your hands in the solvent afterwards","Leave the lid open to let fumes escape","Wear gloves and keep the lid closed when not in use"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"Solvents harm skin and give off vapour. Lids reduce evaporation and fire risk."},
    {"id":"m02-q12","q":"Why should the chuck key be removed before switching on a pedestal drill?","options":["It would scratch the workpiece and spoil the finish of the part","It could be thrown out at high speed and cause injury","It would change the drill speed when the motor starts up","It could jam in the drill's gearbox and burn out the motor only"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"A key left in the chuck can be flung out violently. Always remove it before starting."},
    {"id":"m02-q13","q":"Why must air tools be disconnected before you change the tool or work on the hose?","options":["The compressor needs to cool down between uses","The tool will not accept a new attachment under pressure","The oil inside the tool needs time to drain out","A trapped supply could start the tool or whip the hose"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"Compressed air still in the line can start the tool or whip a loose hose. Isolate the supply and release the pressure first."},
    {"id":"m02-q14","q":"Why must the water in a compressor tank be drained every day?","options":["It keeps the pressure in the tank higher than before","Water causes rust in the tank and ruins air tools","The compressor runs faster when the tank is empty of water","The water makes the air hotter and so gives more power"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"Compressed air carries moisture that collects in the tank. Left alone, it rusts the tank from inside and damages tools."},
    {"id":"m02-q15","q":"Why should the battery be disconnected before welding on a vehicle?","options":["The arc can send a surge through the electronics","The weld would be weaker if the battery were connected","The shielding gas reacts badly with battery acid fumes","The battery would drain too quickly during the weld"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l3","explain":"Welding can create voltage spikes that damage control modules. Disconnecting the battery and protecting modules removes this risk."},
    {"id":"m02-q16","q":"What is a diagnostic trouble code (DTC)?","options":["A serial number printed on the plate that names the engine type","A measurement of the pressure inside one of the cylinders","A reading of the battery voltage while the engine is cranking","A code a module stores that points to a faulty circuit or system"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l4","explain":"A DTC says which circuit or system the module saw a problem in. It points you to tests. It does not by itself prove which part is faulty."},
    {"id":"m02-q17","q":"What does an oscilloscope show that a multimeter usually cannot?","options":["The average temperature of a sensor over an hour","How a signal changes over time","The exact weight of a component in grams","The number of kilometres since the last service"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l4","explain":"A scope plots voltage against time, so it shows the shape, speed and glitches of a signal. A meter shows a single averaged value."},
    {"id":"m02-q18","q":"Which tool would you use to find a small vacuum leak in the intake system without any flames or sprays?","options":["A compression tester","A torque wrench","A smoke machine","A feeler gauge"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l4","explain":"A smoke machine pushes harmless smoke into the system, and it escapes where the leak is. It avoids the fire risk of spraying flammable liquids near a running engine."},
    {"id":"m02-q19","q":"A fault code is stored for an oxygen sensor circuit. What is the best next step?","options":["Replace the sensor at once because the code names it directly","Clear the code and drive on, since a code never needs a repair","Replace the engine control unit first, then check the sensor","Test the sensor, wiring and supply, then decide what is faulty"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l4","explain":"A code points to a circuit. The cause can be the sensor, the wiring, a connector, or an air leak. Check with measurements before you replace anything."},
    {"id":"m02-q20","q":"What is the unit of a micrometer's thimble division on a standard metric micrometer?","options":["0.01 mm","1.00 mm per division","0.50 mm per division","0.10 mm per division"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"The thimble has 50 divisions per 0.5 mm of spindle movement, so each division is 0.01 mm."},
    {"id":"m02-q21","q":"A micrometer sleeve shows 6.5 mm and the thimble reads 21. What is the measurement?","options":["8.60 mm in total","6.50 mm on the scale","6.21 mm in total","6.71 mm"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"6.5 + 0.21 = 6.71 mm. The thimble reading is hundredths of a millimetre."},
    {"id":"m02-q22","q":"Why should you close a micrometer with the ratchet or friction thimble?","options":["To protect the sleeve scale from wear when it is closed","To make the spindle move faster towards the part being measured","To apply the same light pressure each time so readings are consistent","To lock the reading in place so that it can be written down"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"Squeezing by hand can compress the part and give a reading that is too small."},
    {"id":"m02-q23","q":"Which instrument is best for measuring crankshaft end float?","options":["A compression tester","A steel rule","A torque wrench","A dial gauge"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"A dial gauge measures small movement accurately when mounted on a stand."},
    {"id":"m02-q24","q":"What does a compression tester measure?","options":["The voltage that the starter motor draws while it cranks the engine","The oil pressure in the main gallery while the engine is idling","The pressure each cylinder makes while the engine is cranked","The fuel pressure at the fuel rail while the engine is running"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"It screws into a spark plug hole and shows how well the cylinder seals."},
    {"id":"m02-q25","q":"What approximate pressure in psi is 1 bar?","options":["1 psi","0.145 psi","14.5 psi","100 psi"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"1 bar is about 100 kPa, which is roughly 14.5 psi. Do not confuse bar with kPa when reading a gauge."},
    {"id":"m02-q26","q":"What does a feeler gauge measure?","options":["The pressure inside a cylinder while cranking","A small gap such as valve clearance","The speed of a rotating shaft in rpm","The temperature of a part as it heats up in use"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"Feeler gauges are blades of known thickness that slide into a gap."},
    {"id":"m02-q27","q":"A vernier calliper reads 24 mm on the main scale and the 14th line of the 0.02 mm vernier scale lines up. What is the reading?","options":["24.14 mm","38.00 mm","24.28 mm","24.02 mm"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"Whole millimetres 24 plus 14 × 0.02 = 0.28 gives 24.28 mm. The vernier divisions are worth 0.02 mm each."},
    {"id":"m02-q28","q":"A micrometer sleeve shows 12.5 mm and the thimble reads 31. What is the measurement?","options":["12.31 mm","12.81 mm","15.60 mm","43.50 mm"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"Sleeve reading 12.5 mm plus 31 × 0.01 = 0.31 mm gives 12.81 mm. Each thimble division is 0.01 mm."},
    {"id":"m02-q29","q":"What is Plastigauge used for?","options":["Measuring bearing clearance","Measuring the pitch of a damaged thread","Measuring the flatness of a cylinder head","Measuring the hardness of a bolt head"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l5","explain":"The soft plastic thread is crushed between the bearing and the journal when the cap is tightened. The width of the flattened thread is compared with a chart to read the clearance."},
    {"id":"m02-q30","q":"Why should a torque wrench not be used to loosen a fastener?","options":["It only works in the tightening direction because of the ratchet","It will tighten the fastener at the same time","It is too short to turn tight bolts at all","It can damage the mechanism and give wrong readings later"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Loosening can overload the wrench's calibrated mechanism. Use a breaker bar for loosening."},
    {"id":"m02-q31","q":"What is the correct tool to run a wheel nut on quickly and then finish tightening?","options":["An impact wrench to run it on, then a torque wrench for final torque","A pair of pliers on the nut to finish the tightening by hand","An impact wrench for both the running on and the final tightening","A breaker bar for both the running on and the final tightening"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Impact tools are inaccurate for final torque and can overtighten. Use a torque wrench to finish."},
    {"id":"m02-q32","q":"What does torque measure?","options":["The speed of rotation","The thickness of a part","A turning force","A pulling force along a bolt"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Torque is force multiplied by the distance from the axis, in newton metres."},
    {"id":"m02-q33","q":"You tighten a cylinder head with a torque wrench. What is the correct approach?","options":["Use an impact wrench at full speed on all of the bolts","Tighten each bolt fully before you move on to the next bolt","Follow the sequence in the manual and tighten in stages","Tighten from the outside towards the centre in one single pass"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Even tightening in sequence and stages avoids distorting the head and sealing unevenly."},
    {"id":"m02-q34","q":"A manual gives a torque of 30 N·m plus 90° for a bolt. What does this mean?","options":["Tighten to 30 N·m, then turn a further quarter turn","Tighten to a final value of 90 N·m in one single pull","Tighten to 30 N·m and mark a circle 90 mm wide around it","Tighten to 30 N·m, then loosen the bolt by 90 degrees again"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Torque-plus-angle stretches the bolt into a controlled range. Such bolts are normally replaced once removed."},
    {"id":"m02-q35","q":"Why can oil on a bolt thread cause over-tightening?","options":["Less friction means more of the torque becomes bolt stretch","Oil makes the bolt expand when the engine warms up and so it stretches","Oil changes the thread pitch so that the bolt turns further","Oil makes the torque wrench click earlier than it should do"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Torque values assume the specified thread condition. Lubricant lowers friction, so the same torque stretches the bolt further."},
    {"id":"m02-q36","q":"After 50 km, why should wheel nuts be re-checked?","options":["The nuts shrink slightly as they cool down after driving","The brake discs expand at that distance and push the nuts loose","The wheel can settle against the hub and the nuts can loosen","The torque wrench changes its reading after the first use"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"A wheel can bed in after fitting, reducing the clamping load. A re-check confirms the nuts are still tight."},
    {"id":"m02-q37","q":"A torque wrench has an effective length of 0.40 m. With an in-line extension of 0.10 m, what should the wrench be set to for 100 N·m at the bolt?","options":["100 N·m","125 N·m","80 N·m","60 N·m"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"The wrench setting is T × L ÷ (L + E) = 100 × 0.40 ÷ 0.50 = 80 N·m. The extension adds leverage, so the wrench setting must be lower."},
    {"id":"m02-q38","q":"What should you do with a click-type torque wrench after use?","options":["Leave it at the highest torque setting it was used at","Turn it back to its lowest setting","Use it to loosen the last bolts you tightened","Tighten the handle fully until it will not move"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"Leaving the spring under tension weakens it and ruins the accuracy of the wrench. Return it to the lowest setting and store it in its box."},
    {"id":"m02-q39","q":"Why are torque-to-yield bolts not reused?","options":["They are already stretched and can break or lose grip","They are made with a thread that cannot be cleaned","They need the same wrench to be refitted that removed them","They are painted and the paint wears off in use"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l6","explain":"A torque-to-yield bolt is tightened into the stretch range. Removing it leaves it permanently longer and weaker. Reuse risks failure or a leaking joint."},
    {"id":"m02-q40","q":"Why should you take photographs and label hoses and connectors before you dismantle a unit?","options":["It reduces the time the customer is charged for the job","It makes the parts easier to clean in the parts washer","It is required before any torque wrench can be used","It helps you refit everything in the right place"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l7","explain":"Photographs and labels remove guesswork during reassembly, and prevent crossed hoses and wrong connectors."},
    {"id":"m02-q41","q":"Which of these should always be replaced during reassembly of an oil-wet joint?","options":["The main casting of the housing in all cases","The gasket and oil seals","Every fastener, whatever its condition","The torque wrench used for the job"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l7","explain":"Gaskets and seals are compressed and set when fitted, and they do not seal reliably a second time. They are cheap compared to the cost of a leak."},
    {"id":"m02-q42","q":"A bearing is being pressed onto a shaft. Which ring should the press force be applied to?","options":["The inner ring, which fits tightly on the shaft","The outer ring, so the balls are pressed in evenly","The cage between the balls, to push the balls in","Either ring, because they are the same"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l7","explain":"Force must pass through the ring with the interference fit, otherwise it goes through the balls or rollers and damages them. On a shaft that is the inner ring."},
    {"id":"m02-q43","q":"What does the VIN identify?","options":["Only the service history of the vehicle and its last owner","The individual vehicle, including maker, model year and plant","Only the engine code and nothing else about the vehicle at all","Only the paint colour and the trim level of the vehicle"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"The 17-character VIN is unique, so data and parts can be matched to that vehicle."},
    {"id":"m02-q44","q":"Which document gives torque values, clearances and procedures?","options":["The parts catalogue","The workshop manual","The owner's handbook only","The vehicle registration document"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Workshop manuals are written for technicians and give specifications and procedures."},
    {"id":"m02-q45","q":"You need a part number for a worn bracket. Where should you look first?","options":["The parts catalogue with the exploded diagram, using the VIN","The wiring diagram, which shows the circuits and connectors","The service bulletin list, which shows known faults only","The fault-code chart, which lists the codes from the scan tool"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Parts catalogues show each component and its number for a specific vehicle."},
    {"id":"m02-q46","q":"What does a service bulletin provide?","options":["A price list for the parts that the maker sells to dealers","A summary of the road tax rules for the vehicle's market","The warranty terms of a vehicle and how long they last","Notices from the maker about known faults and updated procedures"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Bulletins often contain the fastest solution to a known problem."},
    {"id":"m02-q47","q":"A technician uses a manual for the wrong market of the same model. What is the risk?","options":["The tools will not fit the vehicle","The instructions will be in the wrong language only","The vehicle's warranty will be cancelled automatically","The specifications or parts may not match the vehicle"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Engines and equipment can differ between markets. Check the engine code and year before relying on data."},
    {"id":"m02-q48","q":"What does the tenth character of a 17-character VIN normally indicate?","options":["The country of the owner","The colour of the paint","The number of seats","The model year"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"The tenth position is the model year code. The first three identify the maker and country, and the last six are the serial number."},
    {"id":"m02-q49","q":"Why should you take torque values from the manual for the exact vehicle and not from memory?","options":["Memory is slower than reading a printed manual","The manual is written in a more advanced language","Values depend on the bolt and part, and wrong ones cause damage","Torque values are the same on every vehicle of the same maker and age"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Torque depends on the bolt size and grade, the thread condition and the part. A value remembered from another vehicle can strip a thread or leave a joint loose."},
    {"id":"m02-q50","q":"A forum post gives a torque for a suspension bolt that differs from the workshop manual. What should you do?","options":["Use the manual value and treat the post as doubtful","Use the forum value because it comes from a real user","Use the average of the two values","Tighten until it feels right and skip the check"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l8","explain":"Safety-critical data must come from a reliable source for the exact vehicle. Forum posts can be wrong or for a different model."},
    {"id":"m02-q51","q":"How many millimetres are in 2.5 inches?","options":["63.5 mm","254 mm in total","6.35 mm in total","25.0 mm in total"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"1 in = 25.4 mm, so 2.5 × 25.4 = 63.5 mm."},
    {"id":"m02-q52","q":"A gear with 15 teeth drives a gear with 45 teeth. What is the ratio and what happens to the speed?","options":["1 to 3; the driven gear turns at three times the speed","45 to 15; the driven gear turns at the same speed as before","3 to 1; the driven gear turns at three times the speed","3 to 1; the driven gear turns at one third of the speed"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"Ratio = driven teeth ÷ driving teeth = 3. A larger driven gear turns slower with more torque."},
    {"id":"m02-q53","q":"A cylinder has a swept volume of 450 cm³ and a clearance volume of 50 cm³. What is the compression ratio?","options":["10 to 1","8 to 1 in total","11 to 1 in total","9 to 1 in total"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"CR = (450 + 50) ÷ 50 = 10. Do not forget to add the clearance volume to the top line."},
    {"id":"m02-q54","q":"A cylinder has a bore of 80 mm and a stroke of 90 mm. What is its swept volume?","options":["About 720 cm³","About 225 cm³","About 1,810 cm³","About 452 cm³"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"Swept volume = 0.7854 × 8.0² × 9.0 = 452.4 cm³. Multiplying by four cylinders gives about 1,810 cm³, a 1.8 L engine."},
    {"id":"m02-q55","q":"An engine produces 200 N·m at 3,000 rpm. What is the power?","options":["About 600 kW","About 6.28 kW","About 62.8 kW","About 66.7 kW"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"Power in kW = torque × rpm ÷ 9549 = 200 × 3,000 ÷ 9,549 = 62.8 kW."},
    {"id":"m02-q56","q":"A car uses 8.0 L/100 km. How many kilometres does it travel on one litre?","options":["8.0 km","12.5 km","0.08 km","800 km"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l9","explain":"Divide 100 by the L/100 km figure: 100 ÷ 8.0 = 12.5 km per litre."},
    {"id":"m02-q57","q":"A cylinder bore measures 82.06 mm at the top and 82.01 mm at the bottom. What is the taper?","options":["0.05 mm","0.07 mm","82.01 mm","0.03 mm"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"Taper is the difference between top and bottom readings: 82.06 − 82.01 = 0.05 mm."},
    {"id":"m02-q58","q":"Why measure a bore in more than one place?","options":["To detect taper and out-of-round wear","To find the temperature of the block","To confirm the instrument is switched on","To make the measurement last longer"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"A bore wears unevenly, so one reading can miss the real condition."},
    {"id":"m02-q59","q":"A torque wrench has been dropped. What should you do?","options":["Use it as normal, because the click will still be accurate","Set it to a higher value to make up for the knock","Have it checked against a standard before using it again","Throw it away immediately and buy a new one at once"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"A shock can change the calibration. A tool that is not checked may give wrong torques."},
    {"id":"m02-q60","q":"A technician measures an engine block immediately after it has been running. What problem does this cause?","options":["The micrometer will not close on the hot part at all","Heat expansion can make the bore read larger than when cool","The reading will always be exact because heat is stable","The block will be too clean to measure with any accuracy"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"Metals expand when hot, so measure when the part is cool unless the manual says otherwise."},
    {"id":"m02-q61","q":"What is the best way to carry out a measurement that gives a surprising result?","options":["Accept the reading as it is and order a new part for the job","Check the tool and repeat the measurement before deciding the part is faulty","Round the reading to the nearest millimetre to avoid mistakes","Ask the customer to approve the repair before checking again"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"A dirty anvil, a worn instrument or a wrong zero can create a false result."},
    {"id":"m02-q62","q":"A bore measures 80.04 mm at the top and 80.00 mm at the bottom. What is the taper?","options":["80.04 mm","0.02 mm","160.04 mm","0.04 mm"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"Taper is the difference between the readings at the top and bottom: 80.04 − 80.00 = 0.04 mm. Compare it with the limit in the manual."},
    {"id":"m02-q63","q":"What is the wear limit of a part?","options":["The size of the part when it left the factory as new","The most wear allowed before the part must be replaced","The weight above which the part cannot be lifted by hand","The number of hours the part has been in use"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m02-l10","explain":"The wear limit is the largest wear the manual allows. A reading beyond it means the part is no longer fit for service."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m02-c01","front":"Which socket type is better for a tight bolt, six-point or twelve-point?","back":"A six-point socket grips more of the flats and rounds fewer corners.","lesson":"m02-l1"},
    {"id":"m02-c02","front":"What does M10 × 1.5 mean?","back":"A metric bolt with 10 mm diameter and a thread pitch of 1.5 mm.","lesson":"m02-l1"},
    {"id":"m02-c03","front":"What is a breaker bar used for?","back":"Loosening tight fasteners with extra leverage. It is not for tightening to torque.","lesson":"m02-l1"},
    {"id":"m02-c04","front":"Why is a spanner pulled rather than pushed?","back":"If it slips, your hand moves away from the vehicle.","lesson":"m02-l1"},
    {"id":"m02-c05","front":"What is the safest way to pull a spanner?","back":"Pull it towards you, so that if it slips your hand moves away from the vehicle.","lesson":"m02-l1"},
    {"id":"m02-c06","front":"Describe an M12 × 1.25 thread.","back":"12 mm diameter and 1.25 mm pitch (distance between neighbouring threads). It is a metric fine thread.","lesson":"m02-l2"},
    {"id":"m02-c07","front":"What does the property class 8.8 tell you?","back":"The first number times 100 is the tensile strength in MPa, so about 800 MPa. A higher number means a stronger bolt.","lesson":"m02-l2"},
    {"id":"m02-c08","front":"Name three locking devices for fasteners.","back":"Split pin, self-locking (nyloc) nut, tab washer, spring washer, thread-locking compound.","lesson":"m02-l2"},
    {"id":"m02-c09","front":"What are crush washers and when do you replace them?","back":"Soft copper or aluminium washers that deform to seal. Replace them every time.","lesson":"m02-l2"},
    {"id":"m02-c10","front":"What is an impact wrench used for?","back":"Running fasteners on or off quickly. It is not for final torque.","lesson":"m02-l3"},
    {"id":"m02-c11","front":"How far should the tool rest be from a bench grinder wheel?","back":"No more than about 3 mm.","lesson":"m02-l3"},
    {"id":"m02-c12","front":"Which equipment supports a vehicle after a jack has lifted it?","back":"Axle stands rated for the weight, or a vehicle lift.","lesson":"m02-l3"},
    {"id":"m02-c13","front":"Why drain a compressor tank daily?","back":"Water collects and rusts the tank and tools.","lesson":"m02-l3"},
    {"id":"m02-c14","front":"What does a scan tool read from a vehicle?","back":"Diagnostic trouble codes, freeze-frame data and live data from the control modules.","lesson":"m02-l4"},
    {"id":"m02-c15","front":"What is an oscilloscope used for?","back":"To show how a voltage signal changes over time, so you can see shape, speed and drop-outs.","lesson":"m02-l4"},
    {"id":"m02-c16","front":"What is a smoke machine used for?","back":"To find vacuum, intake and evaporative leaks by seeing where smoke escapes.","lesson":"m02-l4"},
    {"id":"m02-c17","front":"What is an outside micrometer's accuracy?","back":"0.01 mm.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c18","front":"How do you read a micrometer?","back":"Sleeve reading plus thimble reading. For example 7.50 + 0.38 = 7.88 mm.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c19","front":"What does a dial gauge measure?","back":"Small movements such as end float, runout or backlash.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c20","front":"What does a feeler gauge measure?","back":"A small gap such as valve clearance or spark plug gap.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c21","front":"What does a leak-down tester show?","back":"How much cylinder pressure leaks away, and where it escapes.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c22","front":"How many psi in 1 bar?","back":"About 14.5 psi.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c23","front":"How do you read an outside micrometer?","back":"Sleeve reading (whole and half mm) plus thimble reading (each division is 0.01 mm).","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c24","front":"What does Plastigauge measure?","back":"Bearing clearance, from the width of a crushed plastic thread compared with a chart.","lesson":"m02-l5"},
    {"id":"m02-c25","front":"What is torque?","back":"A turning force, measured in newton metres (N·m).","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c26","front":"Why must a torque wrench never be used to loosen bolts?","back":"It can damage the mechanism and lose its calibration.","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c27","front":"What is torque-to-yield?","back":"A bolt tightened with torque plus an angle so it stretches into a controlled range. Use it once only.","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c28","front":"How are wheel nuts tightened correctly?","back":"In a crisscross sequence, in stages, to the full torque, and checked again after about 50 km.","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c29","front":"Formula linking torque, bolt diameter and clamp force?","back":"T = K × D × F, with K about 0.2 for a dry steel thread. Oil lowers K and raises the clamp force.","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c30","front":"Torque wrench setting with an in-line extension?","back":"T_wrench = T_wanted × L ÷ (L + E), where L is the wrench length and E is the extension.","lesson":"m02-l6"},
    {"id":"m02-c31","front":"Where should a bearing press force be applied?","back":"On the ring with the tight fit: inner ring for a shaft, outer ring for a housing.","lesson":"m02-l7"},
    {"id":"m02-c32","front":"Name five parts that should always be replaced during reassembly.","back":"Gaskets, O-rings, oil seals, crush washers, split pins, and single-use or torque-to-yield bolts.","lesson":"m02-l7"},
    {"id":"m02-c33","front":"What is a VIN?","back":"A 17-character code that identifies one vehicle.","lesson":"m02-l8"},
    {"id":"m02-c34","front":"What does a workshop manual contain?","back":"Procedures, specifications, torque values, capacities and fault-finding charts.","lesson":"m02-l8"},
    {"id":"m02-c35","front":"What is a service bulletin?","back":"A notice from the maker about a known fault or updated procedure.","lesson":"m02-l8"},
    {"id":"m02-c36","front":"What do the first three characters of a VIN identify?","back":"The maker and country of origin (world manufacturer identifier).","lesson":"m02-l8"},
    {"id":"m02-c37","front":"What is the formula for compression ratio?","back":"(Swept volume + clearance volume) ÷ clearance volume.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c38","front":"How do you calculate a gear ratio?","back":"Driven teeth divided by driving teeth.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c39","front":"What is 1 inch in millimetres?","back":"25.4 mm.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c40","front":"Swept volume of one cylinder?","back":"π ÷ 4 × bore² × stroke. Multiply by the number of cylinders for engine capacity.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c41","front":"Power from torque and speed?","back":"Power (kW) = torque (N·m) × rpm ÷ 9549.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c42","front":"Convert L/100 km to km per litre.","back":"Divide 100 by the L/100 km figure. 8.0 L/100 km is 12.5 km/L.","lesson":"m02-l9"},
    {"id":"m02-c43","front":"What is bore taper?","back":"The difference between the top and bottom measurements of a worn cylinder bore.","lesson":"m02-l10"},
    {"id":"m02-c44","front":"Why measure engine parts when cool?","back":"Metal expands when hot, which changes the reading.","lesson":"m02-l10"},
    {"id":"m02-c45","front":"What should you record with a measurement?","back":"The value, the unit, the specification and whether it is within limits.","lesson":"m02-l10"},
    {"id":"m02-c46","front":"What is taper in a cylinder bore?","back":"The difference between the readings at the top and bottom of the bore. The top is usually wider.","lesson":"m02-l10"}
  ]
});
